From 8113e5b77854aef14b67c27b5623a8f348c70057 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: facetimehd downstream <facetimehd-downstream@localhost>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +1000
Subject: [PATCH 12/19] v4l2: add NV12, and pass the ISP the real destination
 stride

The ISP's semi-planar output, format code 0, is NV12. Upstream's 2015
comment called it "plane 0 Y plane 1 UV" without giving it a sampling, and
every later reading of this driver filled in 4:2:2 and called it NV16.
Hardware says otherwise: capturing through code 0 gives a width*height luma
plane followed by exactly width*height/2 of chroma - 360 chroma rows for a
720-row frame - with the remaining 460,800 bytes of an NV16-sized buffer
never written. Splitting that chroma gives Cb 122.3 and Cr 135.3 against
122.5 and 135.4 from a YUYV capture of the same scene, and luma 93.1 against
92.9. NV16 is not offered: no output-format code produces it.

NV12 is advertised first and is what a freshly probed device reports, so
ENUM_FMT index 0, what TRY_FMT/S_FMT coerce an unsupported request to, and
what G_FMT returns are all the same value. The measured half of that
argument is size - 1.5 bytes per pixel against 2, so a frame is three
quarters of the packed one in DMA and in every copy after it. The other half
is a judgement about who reads index 0: anything that negotiates walks the
list and names its format, while the simple code that takes index 0 is
mostly feeding a display or a 4:2:0 encoder that would have downsampled the
chroma anyway. YUYV and YVYU are still enumerated and still accepted.

The chroma plane is a byte offset into the same S2 mapping, because
iommu_allocate_sgtable() gives each buffer one contiguous run of IOVA pages,
so it needs no second mapping. The vb2 queue stays single-planar, with the
semi-planar case derived from the pixel format at each use rather than
cached in a plane count - a cached count is what once asked a
V4L2_BUF_TYPE_VIDEO_CAPTURE queue for two planes.

CISP_CMD_CH_OUTPUT_CONFIG_SET's x2 turns out to be the destination row
stride in bytes, not the "chroma size?" it was guessed to be. Upstream
hardcoded width * 2, which for the packed formats is simultaneously a
correct stride and an unremarkable constant, so nothing distinguished the
two readings until a one-byte-per-pixel plane arrived: the ISP then wrote
luma rows 2560 bytes apart into a buffer laid out for 1280, leaving exactly
half the luma plane zero in a strict every-other-row pattern. It raised no
IOMMU fault - the ISP skips rows rather than overrunning, so sizeimage still
covered it - and only inspecting the planes separately found it. The command
now takes the stride and is passed bytesperline; for YUYV and YVYU that is
width * 2 and the command is byte-for-byte what it always was.

The WARN_ON fallback in fthd_start_channel() deliberately does not follow
the default to code 0: it is reached with bytesperline computed for a packed
frame, and the semi-planar layout with a packed stride is the bug above.
